#!/bin/sh
#
# opensearch-plugin against this installation.
#
# Read this before using it. Installing a plugin into <home>/plugins does NOT make it available
# to the embedded node, and can stop the node starting.
#
# OpenSearch's PluginsService scans <home>/plugins and <home>/modules and, for every bundle it
# finds, runs a jar-hell check against System.getProperty("java.class.path"). In this process
# that property describes lib/boot - the supervisor's classpath - and not the OpenSearch child
# ClassLoader's, so the check both misses real conflicts and invents ones that are not there,
# failing startup with "IllegalStateException: jar hell!". OpenSearchService therefore passes
# transport-netty4 as a classpath plugin (PluginInfo) and never populates those directories.
#
# The supported way to add a plugin to this distribution is to put its jars in lib/opensearch/,
# where the child ClassLoader picks them up like every other jar, and to have the runtime pass
# the plugin class as a classpath plugin. That is a change to runtime-opensearch, not something
# this tool can do.
#
# What remains useful is `list` and `remove` against an OpenSearch installation, and inspecting
# a plugin zip before deciding to unpack it into lib/opensearch. The plugin CLI itself ships in
# OpenSearch's distribution/tools/plugin-cli, which is not part of the dependency closure that
# lib/opensearch is assembled from, so this is a passthrough.

set -e

CO_INCLUDE=$(dirname "$0")/cassandra-opensearch.in.sh
[ -r "$CO_INCLUDE" ] || { echo "opensearch-plugin: missing $CO_INCLUDE" >&2; exit 1; }
# shellcheck source=cassandra-opensearch.in.sh
. "$CO_INCLUDE"

co_require_java_21

case "$1" in
    install)
        cat >&2 <<EOF
opensearch-plugin: refusing to install into $CO_HOME/plugins.

The embedded node never reads that directory, and if it exists PluginsService runs a jar-hell
check against the supervisor's classpath rather than OpenSearch's - which fails startup with
"jar hell!" for conflicts that are not real.

To add a plugin here, put its jars in $CO_HOME/lib/opensearch/ and have runtime-opensearch pass
the plugin class as a classpath plugin. See docs/spikes/opensearch-embedding.md.
EOF
        exit 1
        ;;
esac

if [ -n "$OPENSEARCH_TOOLS_HOME" ] && [ -x "$OPENSEARCH_TOOLS_HOME/bin/opensearch-plugin" ]; then
    OPENSEARCH_PATH_CONF="$CO_CONF"
    export OPENSEARCH_PATH_CONF
    exec "$OPENSEARCH_TOOLS_HOME/bin/opensearch-plugin" "$@"
fi

cat >&2 <<EOF
opensearch-plugin: not available in this distribution.

The plugin CLI ships in OpenSearch's distribution/tools/plugin-cli, which is not part of the
org.opensearch:opensearch dependency closure that lib/opensearch is assembled from, so there is
no jar here to run. Point OPENSEARCH_TOOLS_HOME at an OpenSearch installation of the same
version as $(basename "$(ls "$CO_HOME"/lib/opensearch/opensearch-[0-9]*.jar 2> /dev/null | head -1)")
if you need it.
EOF
exit 1
